.TH "PORTUALE\-PORTAGEQ" "1" "Oct 2026" "Portuale @VERSION@" "Portuale"
.SH "NAME"
portuale\-portageq \- Ask the installed\-package database whether a package is installed
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.TP
.B portuale portageq has_version
\fIEROOT\fR \fIATOM\fR
.TP
.B portuale portageq best_version
\fIEROOT\fR \fIATOM\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
A native replacement for the two \fBportageq\fR commands that ebuilds reach
through the \fBhas_version\fR and \fBbest_version\fR helpers.  It runs when
the binary is invoked as \fBportageq\fR or as \fBportuale portageq\fR.  Any
other \fBportageq\fR command is refused with
\fBnot implemented by portuale portageq: \fR\fICMD\fR and exit status 1.
.LP
\fBhas_version\fR prints nothing and exits 0 if an installed package matches
\fIATOM\fR, 1 if none does.  \fBbest_version\fR prints the highest matching
installed \fBcategory/package\-version\fR, or an empty line when nothing
matches, and exits 0.  Matching is the resolver's own: slot, sub\-slot,
version operators, \fB::repo\fR, and USE dependencies checked against the
installed package's recorded USE.
.LP
If the environment variable \fBUSE\fR is set, the USE conditionals of
\fIATOM\fR (\fB[flag?]\fR, \fB[flag=]\fR, ...) are evaluated against it.
If \fBEBUILD_PHASE\fR is set the atom is parsed strictly: an unparsable
atom prints \fBERROR: Invalid atom\fR on standard error and exits 2; an
atom that is invalid only for \fBEAPI\fR draws a \fBQA Notice\fR through
\fBeqawarn\fR (from \fB$PORTAGE_BIN_PATH/isolated\-functions.sh\fR, else
standard error) and is still matched; \fB::repo\fR is refused when
\fBEAPI\fR names a real EAPI.  Without \fBEBUILD_PHASE\fR an unparsable atom
is expanded like a bare package name (\fBglibc\fR finds \fBsys\-libs/glibc\fR);
a malformed one makes \fBhas_version\fR exit 2 and \fBbest_version\fR exit 1.
.SH "BACKEND"
By default the classic tree \fIEROOT\fR\fB/var/db/pkg\fR is read.  When
\fBmrg\fR runs with a database backend it exports \fBPORTUALE_VDB_BACKEND\fR,
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_PATH\fR and \fBPORTUALE_VDB_ROOT\fR to its children.  The
database is then opened read\-only, and used for \fIEROOT\fR equal to
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_ROOT\fR only; any other root (for example \fB/\fR for
\fBhas_version \-b\fR in a chroot build) keeps the files tree.  Without
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_ROOT\fR the database is used for any \fIEROOT\fR.
.LP
\fBredb\fR allows only one process to use the file at a time, and a merging
\fBmrg\fR holds it.  \fBmrg\fR then answers the lookups itself over a Unix
socket in a private directory and exports its path in
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_IPC\fR.  With the \fBredb\fR backend, \fIEROOT\fR equal to
\fBPORTUALE_VDB_ROOT\fR and \fBPORTUALE_VDB_IPC\fR set, every
installed\-package lookup is sent to the parent; the atom rules above are
still applied here.  The parent answers from the last committed state of
the database: during \fBpkg_preinst\fR the instance being replaced is still
installed and the new one is not.  Without \fBPORTUALE_VDB_IPC\fR a
\fBredb\fR file is opened read\-only when no other process holds it.  The
\fBfiles\fR and \fBsqlite\fR backends never use the socket.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
\fBhas_version\fR: installed.  \fBbest_version\fR: always, unless the atom is
invalid.
.TP
.B 1
\fBhas_version\fR: not installed.  Also an unknown command, and
\fBbest_version\fR with a malformed atom.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid atom.
.TP
.B 3
Fewer than two arguments (\fBERROR: insufficient parameters!\fR on standard
output).
.TP
.B 4
The database could not be opened (for example a \fBredb\fR file held by
another process with no \fBPORTUALE_VDB_IPC\fR), or the parent \fBmrg\fR
could not be reached over \fBPORTUALE_VDB_IPC\fR or refused the query.
.TP
.B 64
\fIEROOT\fR is not a directory.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mrg (1),
.BR portuale\-vdb (1),
.BR ebuild (5)
